/// The crate DIRECTORY a record's `src` belongs to, REPO-RELATIVE — `crates/plandb`, `src-tauri`.
///
/// Repo-relative rather than a bare crate NAME under `crates/`, and that distinction is the whole
/// fix. A crate does not have to live there: `src-tauri` is a workspace member ONE level up, so the
/// `crates/` strip this replaces returned `None` for every one of its records. The moment it
/// migrated, all 138 fell out of the Tier-A exclusion and the standalone sweep built whole crate
/// modules alone — MEASURED at `src-tauri-compiles-from-the-graph`: **119 FAILED** with `unresolved
/// import tauri`, with nothing about the records changed. That is the precise libel [`is_tier_a`]
/// exists to prevent, reached through the one input it could not express.
///
/// A directory is also the key nothing can collide in, because it is what a manifest is READ from: a
/// bare name would put a hypothetical top-level `data/` crate and `crates/data` in one bucket, and
/// excluding either would silently exclude the other.
///
/// `scripts/graph-rust-compile.mjs::crateDirOf` is the twin, and the two must move together.
pub fn crate_of(src: Option<&str>) -> Option<&str> {
    let src = src?;
    let idx = src.find("/src/")?;
    Some(&src[..idx]).filter(|s| !s.is_empty())
}
